        "enrichment": {
          "enrichment_status": "ready",
          "enrichment_text": "Notes — A page. — Estuary work rewards patience over instrumentation, and the crews who log the most useful observations are the ones who return to the same bend of water for years rather than sweeping a wider stretch once. The kingfisher audits the riverbank ledger before the tide turns. The bird arrives before the survey team does, works the shallows on a schedule nobody set, and leaves once the water turns over. What the crews write down afterwards is closer to an account of attention than to a measurement, which is why two teams reading the same reach of river rarely produce the same page of notes.",
